{
  "query": "Lumen Outfitters runs five Cloud SQL instances for several internal services. Demand is high from Monday through Friday between 08:00 and 18:00 and it is much lower overnight and on most weekends. You want to reduce spend while still having enough capacity during the busy windows. What should you do?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Migrate to Cloud Spanner and enable autoscaling of compute capacity",
      "explanation": "This option moves the workload to Cloud Spanner and uses node autoscaling to react to changes in traffic.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Configure Cloud Scheduler to invoke a Cloud Function that calls the Cloud SQL Admin API to resize instance machine types before and after peak hours",
      "explanation": "This option programmatically adjusts vCPU and memory for Cloud SQL instances on a schedule that follows predictable demand patterns.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Enable high availability on every Cloud SQL instance and watch utilization through Cloud Monitoring",
      "explanation": "This option provides failover resilience and uses monitoring dashboards to observe performance metrics.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use a managed instance group to automatically scale the Cloud SQL instances up and down",
      "explanation": "This option relies on autoscaling features that are designed for virtual machine instances and not for managed database instances.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Configure Cloud Scheduler to invoke a Cloud Function that calls the Cloud SQL Admin API to resize instance machine types before and after peak hours</b>.</p><p>This works because Cloud SQL does not automatically scale compute. You can programmatically change the machine type through the Admin API which triggers a restart, so scheduling the upsizing just before 08:00 and the downsizing after 18:00 helps contain cost while preserving capacity during business hours. Using a scheduled trigger ensures predictable changes for weekdays and lets you keep smaller, cheaper instances overnight and on weekends.</p><p><i>Migrate to Cloud Spanner and enable autoscaling of compute capacity</i> is not appropriate because Spanner is a different distributed database with different semantics and pricing. Moving from Cloud SQL to Spanner would be a significant migration and is unlikely to reduce cost for typical relational workloads that already fit Cloud SQL.</p><p><i>Enable high availability on every Cloud SQL instance and watch utilization through Cloud Monitoring</i> is incorrect because HA increases instance cost and does not add scaling. Monitoring alone does not change capacity or save money.</p><p><i>Use a managed instance group to automatically scale the Cloud SQL instances up and down</i> is incorrect because managed instance groups apply to Compute Engine virtual machines and cannot manage Cloud SQL instances, which are a fully managed database service.</p>",
  "batch_id": "108",
